 ### [Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md)
-[Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md) is a method for composing multiple SDF primitives into a single implicit surface representing their geometric union. It solves the problem of defining complex scenes without triangle meshes by evaluating the minimum distance from a query point to any primitive in the scene. It builds on [SDF Primitive Definitions](1578-sdf-primitive-definitions.md) by aggregating their outputs [G]. Unlike [Smooth SDF Composition](2685-smooth-sdf-composition.md), which blends surfaces to avoid sharp creases, this method strictly preserves the nearest-surface control required for safe sphere tracing.
+Constructive SDF Modeling constructs a single SDF representing the geometric union of multiple primitives by evaluating the minimum distance to any object in the scene at a query point. It solves the problem of describing complex scenes without triangle meshes, enabling ray marching to step safely toward the nearest surface by guaranteeing that the returned distance is a lower bound on the true distance to the scene geometry. It differs from its parent, [SDF Primitive Definitions](1578-sdf-primitive-definitions.md), by combining individual primitives into a composite field, and from [Boolean Operations on Distance Fields](1961-boolean-operations-on-distance-fields.md) by specifically implementing the union operation (intersection and complement are handled via max and negation respectively).
 
 ### [Signed Distance Field (SDF) Primitive Definition](890-signed-distance-field-sdf-primitive-definition.md)
-A Signed Distance Field (SDF) Primitive Definition is an analytical function $d(\mathbf{p})$ that returns the shortest Euclidean distance from a query point $\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ to the nearest surface of a geometric primitive. The sign of the result indicates the point's position relative to the surface: negative for inside, zero for on the surface, and positive for outside. This method serves as the foundational building block for distance-field ray marching, providing a conservative lower bound on distance that guarantees safe stepping without overshooting. Unlike [Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md) which combines primitives, or [SDF Domain Manipulation Operators](1730-sdf-domain-manipulation-operators.md) which transform space, primitive definitions establish the base geometry itself.
