+### [Offset Surface Evaluation](1782-offset-surface-evaluation.md)
+Offset Surface Evaluation computes the shortest Euclidean distance from a query point $\mathbf{p}$ to a surface generated by offsetting a base "skeleton" geometry $S$ by a fixed distance $c$. It solves the problem of defining implicit surfaces for complex shapes (such as generalized cylinders or space curves) that are difficult to represent with standard primitives. It is distinguished from its siblings by its reliance on a global point-to-set distance search rather than local parametric expansions, which avoids the interior surface artifacts common in local offset methods.
 
 ### [Boolean Operations on Distance Fields](1961-boolean-operations-on-distance-fields.md)
 Boolean operations on Signed Distance Fields (SDFs) combine primitive distance functions to construct complex implicit surfaces using Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) logic. This method solves the problem of modeling arbitrary composite shapes without requiring mesh topology, by evaluating the scalar field of the resulting volume at any point in space. It is distinguished from [Constructive SDF Modeling (Min Combination)](871-constructive-sdf-modeling-min-combination.md) by its strict adherence to exact set-theoretic Boolean logic (min/max) rather than smooth approximations, although smooth variants exist.
 
-#### Offset Surface Evaluation
-- **Definition**: A global offset surface is defined geometrically by the implicit equation $d(p) = 0$, where $d(p)$ is the point-to-set distance to a closed skeleton geometry $S$ [S1]. When $d$ is constant, the surface fleshes out the skeleton [S1]. The point-to-set distance is defined as a search through the entire set for the closest point [S1]. If $S$ has a surface normal defined everywhere, the closest point is one of the points on the surface [S1]. It computes the distance from a point to a surface generated by offsetting a skeleton geometry by a fixed distance [S1].
-- **Parameter**: $c$: constant offset distance applied to the skeleton [S1].
-- **Code**: The closest point on a space curve $\mathbf{p}(t)$ satisfies: $(\mathbf{p}(t) - \mathbf{0}) \cdot \mathbf{p}'(t) = 0$ (Eq. 35) [S1].
-- **Pitfall**: Global offsets can create interior surfaces that cause problems in ray-tracing and CSG operations [S1].
-- **Comparison**: Global offsets are implicit and avoid interior surfaces; local offsets are parametric and can cause gradient discontinuities or high-degree algebraic representations [S1].
